cards.text:
{red|**Take time to name the identity losses you are experiencing.**

What roles did you hold that are now gone or changed?}

{red|How did your spouse reflect your identity back to you?}{red| What did they see in you that you cannot see yourself?}

{red|What future version of yourself died with them?}

{red|How has your place in the social world changed?}

{red|What parts of yourself feel missing or unknown now?}

**Naming these losses is not self-pity.** It is honesty. You cannot rebuild what you have not acknowledged losing.

{red|**Think back to who you were before marriage.**

What did you love to do?}{red| Hobbies, activities, ways of spending time that brought you joy?}

{red|Who were your friends?}{red| People you spent time with before your social life became couple-focused?}

{red|What were your dreams?}{red| Ambitions and hopes you had for yourself, before your dreams merged with someone else's?}

{red|What were you good at?}{red| Skills and strengths that were recognized before your spouse entered the picture?}

{red|What made you you?}{red| The quirks, preferences, personality traits that defined you?}

**Some of these may have continued into your marriage.** Others may have faded. But they are part of who you are, a foundation beneath the identity you built with your spouse.

**They can be a starting point now.** Not to go backward, but to remember that you have existed as a complete person before. You can again.

**"Remember the days of old; consider the generations long past" ({red|Deuteronomy 32:7|Remember the days of old; consider the generations long past}).** Remember your own past. There is wisdom and identity there.

{red|**Spend time remembering your pre-marriage self.**

What hobbies or interests did you have that faded during marriage?}

{red|What friendships from before marriage have you lost touch with?}

{red|What dreams did you have for yourself that got set aside?}

{red|What personality traits or preferences were more prominent before marriage?}

{red|What did you love that you have not done in years?}

{red|Are there any of these that might be worth revisiting now?}{red| Not to recreate the past, but to reconnect with parts of yourself that still exist?}

{red|**Reflect on what your spouse gave you that you can carry forward.**

What character qualities did they help develop in you?}

{red|What skills or abilities did you gain through their influence?}

{red|What did they believe about you that you can choose to believe about yourself?}

{red|What challenges did they bring that ultimately made you better?}

{red|How would they want you to continue growing in these areas?}

**These are the ways they live on in you.** Carrying them forward honors their memory.

{red|**Reflect on who you are becoming through this grief.**

What strengths have you discovered that you did not know you had?}

{red|How has your capacity for compassion grown?}

{red|What do you understand now that you did not understand before?}

{red|How have your priorities shifted?}{red| What matters more now?}{red| What matters less?}

{red|What qualities are developing in you through this process?}

**These are the contours of the person you are becoming.** Not the person you planned to be, but the person you are actually becoming.

{red|**Reflect on the identity truths God speaks over you.**

Which truth about your identity in God is hardest to believe right now?}

{red|Which truth is most comforting?}

{red|How might your sense of self be different if you really believed these truths?}

{red|What identity lies have you been believing?}{red| "I am nothing without my spouse." "I have no value alone." "I do not matter."

How can you replace those lies with the truth of who God says you are?}

**Returning to these truths regularly can rebuild identity on a foundation that cannot be shaken.**

{red|You may have asked yourself: Why am I still here when they are not?}

{red|**Your spouse was good, loved, valuable.** Why did they die while you remain?}{red| What is the point of continuing without them?}

{red|**These are not morbid questions.** They are human questions, the natural response to catastrophic loss. When the person you built your life around is gone, it makes sense to wonder about the point of the life that remains.

**George asked himself this every day for months after his wife died.** "She was the better one. She should be here. I do not understand why I am the one left."

**There may not be an answer to why.** But there can be an answer to what for. What is this remaining life for?}{red| What purpose can you find in the days you have been given?}

**"For to me, to live is Christ and to die is gain" ({red|Philippians 1:21|**There may not be an answer to why.** But there can be an answer to what for. What is this remaining life for?}{red| What purpose can you find in the days you have been given?} **}).** Paul grappled with the same tension. He wanted to be with Christ, but he stayed for the sake of purpose. There was work yet to do.

**You are still here.** There must be a reason, even if you cannot see it yet.

{red|**Explore where purpose might be found for you.**

Who needs you?}{red| Family, friends, others who benefit from your presence?}

{red|What unfinished work do you have?}{red| Professional, personal, relational?}

{red|What might you create or contribute?}{red| Skills, gifts, experiences you could share?}

{red|How might your suffering equip you to help others?}{red| Who could you serve because of what you have been through?}

{red|What would your spouse want your purpose to be?}{red| What would they say if they could encourage you toward something?}

{red|What has God been stirring in you?}{red| Any hints of direction, even small ones?}

{red|**Think about what you might want to explore.**

What have you always been curious about but never tried?}

{red|What doors have opened that were closed before?}

{red|What would you try if you were not afraid?}

{red|What would your spouse encourage you to explore?}{red| They knew you. What would they say?}

{red|What feels slightly uncomfortable but also slightly exciting?}

**Make a list of possibilities.** You do not have to pursue all of them. But naming them opens the door to exploring.

{red|**Think about how you want to be known.**

What aspects of yourself do you want people to see, beyond your loss?}

{red|How do you want to introduce yourself?}

{red|What conversations do you want to have that are not about grief?}

{red|What relationships would help you feel like a whole person?}

{red|How can you gently resist being reduced to a label?}

**You have the right to define yourself.** Start considering how you want that definition to look.

{red|**Consider how your loss might eventually become a source of meaning.**

What have you learned through grief that could help others?}

{red|Who might benefit from your experience?}

{red|What ways of serving appeal to you?}{red| Being present with grieving people, mentoring, advocacy, service?}

{red|How did your spouse live that you could continue or honor through service?}

{red|Do you feel ready to serve, or do you need more time to heal first?}

**There is no pressure.** Just openness to how meaning might emerge from your pain.

{red|**Dare to imagine who you might become.**

One year from now, what do you hope will be true about your sense of identity?}

{red|Five years from now, who do you imagine you might be?}

{red|What qualities do you hope will characterize you?}{red| What kind of person do you want to become?}

{red|How will you carry your spouse's influence forward in who you become?}

{red|What would make your spouse proud of the person you are becoming?}

**This is not certain.** The future is unknown. But hope gives direction to becoming.
